WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.020
Film Courage: You had written many short
screenplays before? Adam Davis, Writer/Director: Yes. Film Courage: I'm not sure how

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:10.679
many features you'd written but was your
process the same for all of them? Adam: My

00:00:10.679 --> 00:00:17.100
process changed a bit as I was going
there was definitely a phase in my

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.150
mid-20s where I brought out the cork
board and started doing like the cue

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.660
cards you know on the board like all
right here's act one here the scenes and

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:31.320
here's act 2 etc and I sort of fell away
from that because what I found and this

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.160
is the process that I found on broken
ceiling that really worked for me was

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:42.540
just going into a word doc and making a
very descriptive outline I think the

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:48.239
outline for the film was about 20 pages
and I would I would write like 1 through

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:54.360
10 what's happening there 11 through 20
and just break it sort of down you know

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.920
because I needed to make sure that the
audience was kept on their toes

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.910
throughout the entire thing so I needed
to know okay like what's going on in

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:07.770
this 10-minute sequence what's going on
in the next 20 minutes how do I make

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:12.000
that different from that how do I make
these twists like where does this come

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.630
into thing like making it so that it's
the most dramatic and enjoyable thing

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:24.270
that it can be but I found and that's
what I've used ever since it's like just

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:30.829
making raw almost prewriting the movie
which is interesting I'll just you know

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:35.790
freetype and and write everything
sometimes there's dialogue included

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:39.420
sometimes there's not sometimes it's
just descriptions but if dialogue comes

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.530
out write that down it's a good starting
point but I just go through and you know

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.299
they range from 10 to 20 to sometimes 30
pages based on the project or how long

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:54.119
it is but I really like writing out the
movie in full so I can have that word

00:01:54.119 --> 00:01:58.680
doc open as I'm writing the script and
sort of like compare and go okay this is

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.950
working this is working or changing
things if if I find that something

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:06.030
doesn't work all of a sudden do you
write most of your projects with will

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:11.099
yeah most of them will and I have
written how did you meet well we met at

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:14.870
the at the end
ship at the production company that I

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:19.040
worked for and it was through our you
know working together at the company

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.960
that we forged this creative bond and
you know it was early on and we've just

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:28.790
sort of continued says so when you let's
suppose you meet up I don't know for

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:33.740
coffee or how you discuss ideas but how
do you know that an idea is viable that

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.460
it's actually gonna last through the
whole process hmm

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:48.350
I think that if it excites me or excites
us that's a good starting point is it an

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:54.770
idea that I haven't heard of before is
it something that perhaps is topical or

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:59.390
perhaps is something that had been done
before but has fallen out of fashion and

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:04.580
could use a resurgence you know like
what's it's thinking about like you know

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:09.200
keeping your finger on the pulse what's
gonna be current now but also because

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:15.170
film takes so long to get done what's
gonna be still relevant like three years

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:20.989
from now and sometimes you can't gauge
that and you can't know that so in terms

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:28.610
of viability for me it's really boils
down to like what I need to say you know

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:33.260
like what it what is current in me and
what's interesting in that is that when

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:38.390
I and we have followed that we come up
with things like no touching that you

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:44.209
know back in 2016 when that came out
this was before the me to movement right

00:03:44.209 --> 00:03:49.550
but it was sort of like in that realm of
just seeing where the wind was blowing

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:55.220
broken ceilings sort of same thing like
made that before like everybody else had

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:59.660
in a way caught on and had become a
bigger thing so not to say that we're

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:03.890
you know like predicting the future but
it's just seeing what's going on and

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:08.959
sort of catching those early you know
that early wind of something and then if

00:04:08.959 --> 00:04:17.359
it resonates with us going on and and
pushing it through if it can excite us

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:21.020
and hold our attention because that's
the thing sometimes you'll get an idea

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:25.900
and it's good for the moment and then
you realize that you can't come

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:31.479
everything that you need to commit to it
and then it can sort of like fall away

00:04:31.479 --> 00:04:37.030
